      "id": "12-the-whole-journey",
      "slug": "the-whole-journey",
      "part": "II",
      "partTitle": "Make the thing make sense",
      "order": 12,
      "title": "Think in the whole journey",
      "summary": "Product marketing is a stack of questions covering how people find you, how they start, why they stay, and how you get paid. Acquisition is only the first layer.",
      "readMin": 9,
      "actionMin": 10,
      "takeaway": "If you only measure new visitors, you will not notice that people arrive, get confused, and leave.",
      "minAction": "Write what “started successfully” would mean for your product in one sentence. It should be something a person did, not something you posted.",
      "minActionOutput": "One definition of a successful start.",
      "exercise": "Fill five boxes acquisition, onboarding, engagement or retention, and monetization, plus one note on what you will not measure yet. Write questions, not programs.",
      "exerciseMin": 10,
      "exerciseOutput": "A five-box journey sketch made of questions.",
      "stopWhen": "You can name the layers of the journey, and you have defined what a successful first use looks like.",
      "optional": false,
      "path": "content/lessons/12-the-whole-journey.md",
      "moduleId": "II",
      "moduleTitle": "Make the thing make sense",
      "html": "<h1 id=\"user-content-think-in-the-whole-journey\">Think in the whole journey</h1>\n<p>About 9 minutes to read. Optional exercise about 10 minutes. These are estimates.</p>\n<p>A launch post can bring people to the door. Product marketing asks what happens after they walk in.</p>\n<p>Kunal Gandhi says crypto teams should take a product-marketing approach from the start. The portable version does not require a crypto product. It is a stack of questions whose answers become product work, not only ads.</p>\n<p>He writes the stack as acquisition, onboarding, engagement and retention, and monetization. The same sequence appeared in his earlier list of missteps: teams skip this approach, and many should have started with smoother onboarding.</p>\n<p>In a first-week plan he adds that you should understand the full journey with the people who build the product. Marketing is not a separate department that receives a finished object.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-acquisition\">Acquisition</h2>\n<p>This layer is how the right people find you. Kunal’s notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Define the intended user. Writing a full profile is even better.</li>\n<li>Pick channels.</li>\n<li>Map content loops and campaigns.</li>\n<li>Line up partnerships or integrations that already sit near those users.</li>\n<li>Consider a referral program only if a happy user would naturally tell someone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice what is missing: “be everywhere.” Channel choice still follows the one-channel lesson.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-onboarding\">Onboarding</h2>\n<p>Onboarding is the path from “I arrived” to “I get it.”</p>\n<p>Decide what “onboarded” means in observable terms. In Kunal’s crypto examples that might be a connected wallet or a first transaction. In a more ordinary product it might be “invited one housemate” or “imported an existing list” or “got a decided dinner on the first night.”</p>\n<p>Then design the first moment the product proves itself. If that moment is buried behind a tour, a survey, and a settings page, people will leave before they understand you.</p>\n<p>This is why homepage copy and onboarding are related. The promise you made on the first screen has to come true quickly inside the product.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-engagement-and-retention\">Engagement and retention</h2>\n<p>Kunal writes that acquisition is easy and retention is hard. In another post he says product shelf life is now short, and that most of a marketer’s time should go into how people stay.</p>\n<p>Questions that belong here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Which part of the product would make someone come back without a reminder?</li>\n<li>How does a casual user become someone who relies on it?</li>\n<li>Why do people leave, and what would a honest win-back look like?</li>\n<li>What can you measure without building a warehouse of dashboards on day one?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>His examples include points, drops, and on-chain metrics because he is writing to crypto teams. Translate the questions, not the props. A household list app might care about “second shop completed this week,” not about points.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-monetization\">Monetization</h2>\n<p>Write down why you would charge, how, and for what. Kunal notes that crypto culture often loves free products, and that sustainable revenue still has to be thought about early.</p>\n<p>You do not need a clever pricing page this week. You do need to stop pretending the question can wait forever. Free can be a real strategy. Accidental free, with no idea how the work continues, is just a delay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-a-concrete-example\">A concrete example</h2>\n<p>For the shared shopping list:</p>\n<p>Acquisition might be search for “shared grocery list” plus a card that movers or housemates already hand each other.</p>\n<p>Onboarding might mean “two people checked the same list.” The first proof is seeing someone else grab milk before you leave the house.</p>\n<p>Retention might mean the list is still used on the third shop, not that you sent a streak notification. Monetization might be a family plan, or it might honestly stay free until you know what people would pay for.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-hypothetical-maal-example\">Hypothetical Maal example</h2>\n<p>This example is invented for teaching.</p>\n<p>Acquisition: people who already describe dinner-time shutdown as the problem. Onboarding: a decided dinner on night one, without building a pantry profile first. Retention: they came back the next hard evening without being nagged by a streak. Monetization: whatever model does not turn dinner into another chore to maintain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-what-to-do\">What to do</h2>\n<p><strong>Under 2 minutes.</strong> Write what “started successfully” means, as something a person did.</p>\n<p><strong>Optional, about 10 minutes.</strong> Fill four boxes (acquisition, onboarding, retention, monetization) with questions, plus one note on what you will not measure yet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-youre-done-when\">You're done when</h2>\n<p>You can name the layers, and you have defined a successful first use in plain words.</p>",
