      "id": "opt-one-person-creative-team",
      "slug": "one-person-creative-team",
      "part": "optional",
      "partTitle": "Optional depth",
      "order": 109,
      "title": "The one-person creative team path",
      "summary": "Dan Koe argues that capturing attention is the skill underneath most digital businesses, and that one person can be their own creative team or someone else’s. That path is optional and is not required to finish this course.",
      "readMin": 8,
      "actionMin": 8,
      "takeaway": "Brand strategy, content strategy, and drafting are different jobs. Most people only do the third and then wonder why nothing sticks.",
      "minAction": "Write whether you are more interested in using these skills on your own product, or in selling them. “Neither, not now” is a valid answer.",
      "minActionOutput": "One choice, including “not now.”",
      "exercise": "Using Koe’s three jobs, write one sentence each for brand strategy, content strategy, and drafting as they would apply to your practice idea. Do not start an agency.",
      "exerciseMin": 8,
      "exerciseOutput": "Three sentences.",
      "stopWhen": "You can separate this optional career path from the core beginner course, and you have labeled Koe’s income range as his claim.",
      "optional": true,
      "path": "content/optional/opt-one-person-creative-team.md",
      "moduleId": "optional",
      "moduleTitle": "Optional depth",
      "html": "<h1 id=\"user-content-the-one-person-creative-team-path\">The one-person creative team path</h1>\n<p>About 8 minutes to read. Optional exercise about 8 minutes. These are estimates.</p>\n<p>Dan Koe’s 2027 letter argues that the most lucrative skill has always been capturing attention. Software, books, and career skills do not get paid if nobody knows they exist. Other people, he writes, will also pay you to capture attention for them.</p>\n<p>Mogul later shared a condensed version of that argument. Dan authored it.</p>\n<p>He describes two versions of a one-person creative team: run those skills on your own brand, or become the creative team for founders who do not have time. He mentions income in the range of $10 to $75 thousand a month for one to three clients if you are good and you practice, and he cites large creator-economy totals. Those figures are his claims and citations, not a beginner target, and not a reason to quit the product path in this course.</p>\n<p>Inside the work he splits three jobs people often mash together:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brand strategy: positioning and perception. This is the person who says you should not chase every trend.</li>\n<li>Content strategy: what is working in the topic, which formats to try, what to make next.</li>\n<li>Drafting and publishing: the actual posts, scripts, and letters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most people, he writes, skip the first, half-do the second, and ask an assistant to pump out the third. That is the competition, and it is not hard to stand out by doing the first job.</p>\n<p>He also describes interviewing a client to build their “intellectual signature,” and he promotes Eden, his own tool, plus a paid writing workshop. Those are his products. They are not assignments.</p>\n<p>If this path interests you, the core course already taught the pieces you would sell: interviews, positioning, one idea per post, and a vehicle you can keep. If it does not interest you, skip the rest. You do not need an agency to market something you are building.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-what-to-do\">What to do</h2>\n<p><strong>Under 2 minutes.</strong> Choose “my product,” “other people’s brands,” or “not now.”</p>\n<p><strong>Optional, about 8 minutes.</strong> Write one sentence each for the three jobs. Do not start an agency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-content-youre-done-when\">You're done when</h2>\n<p>You can tell this career path apart from the core course, and you have labeled the income range as his claim.</p>",
