        "url": "/lessons/competitor-ads",
        "excerpt": "A paid ad is a message someone already paid to show buyers. You can study the problem it opens with, the offer, and the proof without sending anyone a message.",
        "searchText": "Read competitor ads as research A paid ad is a message someone already paid to show buyers. You can study the problem it opens with, the offer, and the proof without sending anyone a message. Other people’s ads are a public record of which problems they think are worth paying to name. That is research. Turning those lines into unsolicited DMs is a different, riskier business. From an ad you already remember, write the problem it opened with, what it offered, and any proof it showed. Using Din’s research questions, outline how you would study three competitors’ ads problem, offer, proof. Then, if you want, draft one message you will not send, and write why unsolicited outreach can violate platform rules and annoy people. You can separate ad-library research from outreach, and you have practiced only the research half. What to look at The half we will not assign A concrete example What to do You're done when LinkedIn’s ad library lets anyone inspect ads a company ran. Look at the problem the ad opens with, what it offers, and what proof it uses. Those paid lines are candidates for later messaging tests. Din describes turning those ads into cold LinkedIn messages, sending them at volume, and charging for booked calls, including a $500 test of 1,000 messages. Din’s post frames this as a path to about $20,000 a month. That is a pitch, not evidence from this corpus."
