        "url": "/lessons/video-restraint",
        "excerpt": "Leo’s Apple breakdown is about restraint one idea per shot, controlled color, brand type. Eian’s film workflow locks story and stills before anyone generates motion.",
        "searchText": "Make video look expensive by showing less Leo’s Apple breakdown is about restraint one idea per shot, controlled color, brand type. Eian’s film workflow locks story and stills before anyone generates motion. An expensive-looking video is usually a sequence of clear stills, not a pile of effects. Write one sentence for a product moment, and name the one object that should carry all the color. Sketch three stills for a 15-second product moment first frame, middle action, last frame. Apply Leo’s restraint no extra objects, at most three colors. Do not generate or publish a video. You can explain why story and stills come before motion, and you have a three-still sketch. Restraint on camera Story, then stills, then motion What to do You're done when Apple-style product video uses simple composition (one idea per shot), a tight type system, and controlled color one loud object, a quiet room, rarely bright on bright. Give yourself rules such as three colors, similar backgrounds, and consistent music for a series, so the work looks like it came from one place. Eian’s workflow locks the story, then the storyboard, then style sheets, then first and last frames, then prompts, then motion. Every second should look good as a still."
