        "excerpt": "Dan Koe argues that capturing attention is the skill underneath most digital businesses, and that one person can be their own creative team or someone else’s. That path is optional and is not required to finish this course.",
        "searchText": "The one-person creative team path Dan Koe argues that capturing attention is the skill underneath most digital businesses, and that one person can be their own creative team or someone else’s. That path is optional and is not required to finish this course. Brand strategy, content strategy, and drafting are different jobs. Most people only do the third and then wonder why nothing sticks. Write whether you are more interested in using these skills on your own product, or in selling them. “Neither, not now” is a valid answer. Using Koe’s three jobs, write one sentence each for brand strategy, content strategy, and drafting as they would apply to your practice idea. Do not start an agency. You can separate this optional career path from the core beginner course, and you have labeled Koe’s income range as his claim. What to do You're done when Whatever you build, capturing attention and acquiring customers sit near the center of whether it works. Other people will also pay for that skill. A one-person creative team combines brand strategy (positioning and what you will not chase), content strategy (what is working and what to make next), and drafting or publishing. Koe writes that $10 to $75 thousand a month for one to three clients is not far out of reach for someone good at this, and cites large creator-economy figures. Those are his claims and citations."
